The operator reframed strategy (2026-06-21): stop offline-optimizing; LEARN each unseen game at live
runtime within the 12h/10-steps-sec budget (internal sim is FREE -> learn a transition model from a few
real probes, plan for free, commit the short path, give up gracefully banking each reached level).

Built standalone (low-collision, the conductor wires it in later, NOT this pass):
- python/carnot/agentic/arc_live_ttt.py: LiveTTTWorldModel -- per-game world model learned from played
  transitions, exposing the exact engine(grid,action,data)->grid + is_level_complete contract the
  EXISTING WorldModelVerifier trust gate + plan_in_model BFS consume. L0 exact table + L1 ObjectDeltaModel.
- scripts/arc_ttt_validate.py: offline learning-curve gate (collect_transitions -> train/held-out ->
  learned-vs-LLM held-out accuracy). De-risks BEFORE any submission. 5 unit tests green.
- Codebase finding: the learner was ALREADY fit in-core (_fit_dsl_model) but UNUSED for planning -- the
  planner still calls the failing LLM engine (arc_competition_agent.py:1417). The fix is to make the
  learned model the engine.

HONEST GATE RESULT (the gate did its job): 0/3 games clear the 0.5 trust gate. Diagnostic: the rule
learner predicts 100% of NO-OPs and 0% of state-CHANGING transitions (cd82 0/32, ar25 0/30) on novel
held-out states -- the ObjectDeltaModel hypothesis class genuinely can't express these mechanics. Caught
offline before any submission, exactly as designed.

REDIRECT: this points at the operator's greenlit neural learned-dynamics backend (small grid->grid
CNN / TRM refiner, build step 7) -- now the PRIMARY path, gated by the same harness. Full write-up:
docs/research-notes/arc-live-ttt-2026-06-21.md.

